            <ArticleTitle>Hipertriglyceridemia induced acute pancreatitis in pregnancy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>275-277</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0214-9168(17)30101-8</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.arteri.2017.06.004</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Hypertrigliceridemia is the third most common cause of acute pancreatitis. The risk of developing acute pancreatitis is 5% in healthy patients and 4% during pregnancy with triglyceride levels &gt;1,000mg/dl. During pregnancy there are changes in the lipid profile that increase between two and four times triglyceride levels. Its increase in excessive form produces an oxidative environment with injury of the endothelium and appearance of complications such as preeclampsia or pancreatitis. We present the case of a pregnant woman with pancreatitis secondary to hypertriglyceridemia.</AbstractText>
